{"version":2,"kind":"Article","sha256":"6b63696bca51cbcdefd3465d8044f045f7d8e0896fa46ea0d49b092ff9c02300","slug":"why-study","location":"/introduction/why_study.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Why Study?","authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Eric Araujo","given":"Eric","family":"Araujo"},"name":"Eric Araujo","id":"contributors-why_study-generated-uid-0"}],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"thumbnail":"/woman-05edf11bd3d7abc85abdf9fcdf9c31a6.jpg","thumbnailOptimized":"/woman-05edf11bd3d7abc85abdf9fcdf9c31a6.webp","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"why_study.md","url":"/why_study-d418ec726ca1a4a2fc82501c39d2bbf1.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"comment","value":" | ![](figures/woman.jpg) | ![](figures/VaticanMuseums_148.jpg) | ","key":"cm7PvQSDAM"},{"type":"comment","value":" [Source](https://chandlerozconsultants.wordpress.com/category/sacraments/) ","key":"ZnBZGbCZ2m"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"📜 Lecture Notes","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"SVyb8kSxqJ"}],"key":"hzi6Jkilax"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It was a great surprise to start this course with the question of what difference it makes if we stand, kneel, or sit to pray. The posture we assume when we pray is not a trivial matter. It reflects our understanding of who God is and who we are in relation to Him.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"DJdz9ucL6x"}],"key":"Phg4ewXQIU"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"He also presented the three postures for prayer in the ancient church (and guess what? sitting was not one of them!): standing, kneeling, and prostrating. Each posture has its own meaning and significance:","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"TyKhobNzfN"}],"key":"PS4ldNK9PS"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standing is a posture of respect and attentiveness,","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"IReJQldmfo"}],"key":"mczg4LXDtk"}],"key":"JWI2IxMta3"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kneeling is a posture of humility and submission, and","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"vpepSGYCLj"}],"key":"PJcs6su6j1"}],"key":"W4nDHWQdsV"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prostrating is a posture of total surrender and worship, also connected to repentance.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"ZK8N47QFZf"}],"key":"aYQuvX81uH"}],"key":"STaGGqdQBp"}],"key":"aSVmdziXaq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prof. Rylaarsdam also explained the origins of the expression “The Lord be with you”, recalling Boaz’s greeting to his workers in Ruth 2:4. This greeting is not just a polite expression; it carries deep theological significance. It acknowledges God’s presence and blessing in our lives, especially in communal worship settings.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"LnNH2P0ADf"}],"key":"zXk76XaDBM"}],"icon":false,"key":"KMzBlLe5Pv"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/woman-05edf11bd3d7abc85abdf9fcdf9c31a6.jpg","alt":"Source","width":"300px","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"MPdeNlq4Qz","urlSource":"figures/woman.jpg","urlOptimized":"/woman-05edf11bd3d7abc85abdf9fcdf9c31a6.webp"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://chandlerozconsultants.wordpress.com/category/sacraments/","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"NC9s9ZGCtf"}],"urlSource":"https://chandlerozconsultants.wordpress.com/category/sacraments/","key":"IF6DU7zLEa"}],"key":"VbwVzVH0qL"}],"key":"w0JTcgE20S"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"s8r1cSkCnG"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/VaticanMuseums_148-5f9e1d26c8ebf1c9e57a5e5bae08c283.jpg","alt":"\"Grand Pastoral Sarcophagus\" from Via Prenestina, painted and gilt, late 3rd/early 4th c. Vatican Museums, 31485 (ex 150)","width":"300px","align":"center","key":"oWMyaomAED","urlSource":"figures/VaticanMuseums_148.jpg","urlOptimized":"/VaticanMuseums_148-5f9e1d26c8ebf1c9e57a5e5bae08c283.webp"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"“Grand Pastoral Sarcophagus” from Via Prenestina, painted and gilt, late 3rd/early 4th c. Vatican Museums, 31485 (ex 150) |\n","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"ZruVAfaCyU"},{"type":"link","url":"https://www.kornbluthphoto.com/VaticanPastoralSarcophagus31485.html","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"HbliFt8X1y"}],"urlSource":"https://www.kornbluthphoto.com/VaticanPastoralSarcophagus31485.html","key":"G20BTKSkhZ"}],"key":"w0CKvy7Olz"}],"key":"cBcv6Q1vvI"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"U6zr8XN8OS"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introduction","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"CbPNcZB7eg"}],"identifier":"introduction","label":"Introduction","html_id":"introduction","implicit":true,"key":"Zy705FpsKV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The main question of this topic will be: Why study the history of Christianity? Or as stated by Prof. Rylaarsdam, “What difference does it make to know church history?”","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"aTEzQ7qwnk"}],"key":"BK8UzEEIs1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is quite common for seminary students to question the relevance of church history for their ministry. Some may see it as a mere academic exercise, while others may view it as a distraction from more practical concerns. However, studying church history is essential for several reasons, which will be explored in this topic.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"TA5fIEx0P1"}],"key":"dCSdfklcP2"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/stpeter-d9dfcf435de44ef1b1ad19ab9fc50825.jpg","alt":"St Peter Preaching in the Presence of St Mark. Fra Angelico, c. 1440-41. Source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"NTBErgxYsE","urlSource":"/figures/stpeter.jpg","urlOptimized":"/stpeter-d9dfcf435de44ef1b1ad19ab9fc50825.webp"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"St Peter Preaching in the Presence of St Mark. Fra Angelico, c. 1440-41. ","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"RvtaM0BPTi"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fra_Angelico#","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"tisilk0co8"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fra_Angelico#","data":{"page":"Fra_Angelico#","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"hzrcP9Bd6f"}],"key":"mc8JqHlkJq"}],"key":"oAKf3MJCkj"}],"enumerator":"3","key":"ZRs5fGmJlu"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reasons to Study Church History","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"ggK9R0Fwu2"}],"identifier":"reasons-to-study-church-history","label":"Reasons to Study Church History","html_id":"reasons-to-study-church-history","implicit":true,"key":"UFuFPVMXL2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To summarize, here are some of the key reasons to study church history:","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"wNK974xCJG"}],"key":"pfq8TOWoyQ"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To understand our identity as Christians","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"PWxdXlIitt"}],"key":"qVWmRSKaN9"},{"type":"text","value":": Church history helps us to see how our faith has been shaped and formed over the centuries. It reminds us that we are part of a larger story, a story that goes back to the early church and continues to this day.","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"Cmx8Xqekzb"}],"key":"C7Za7QYYfC"}],"key":"QNHin65ibL"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To learn from the past","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"Iac9I1YheI"}],"key":"q896NoplAr"},{"type":"text","value":": By studying church history, we can learn from the successes and failures of those who came before us. We can see how they navigated challenges and controversies, and we can apply those lessons to our own lives and ministries.","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"ROcZalDHDA"}],"key":"FNR3liVmLX"}],"key":"OP80UqbTHM"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To appreciate the diversity of the Christian tradition","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"xbzyyHUgab"}],"key":"MfK58qImu3"},{"type":"text","value":": Church history reveals the rich diversity of the Christian tradition. It shows us how different cultures and contexts have shaped the way we understand and practice our faith.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"YC2VctYmni"}],"key":"MfIAKFKXD8"}],"key":"bnKVthcGqL"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To deepen our faith","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"mtXWuHr10C"}],"key":"b1d4gLSuez"},{"type":"text","value":": Studying church history can deepen our faith by helping us to see the faithfulness of God throughout the ages. It reminds us that we are part of a larger story of God’s work in the world.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"ytN9YWfLff"}],"key":"fH3rDKvHK5"}],"key":"imb686KN3A"}],"key":"KhJrLm8S6N"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Church history forms Christians for a missional encounter with culture as we observe how the church in the past did that. It is not just an academic exercise but a practical one that equips us to live out our faith in the world today.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"CJ7DsQOT9s"}],"key":"Vh716n8wFu"}],"key":"eJADEV2mzv"}],"key":"rw29Q7uYf7"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}}}